<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali ibn Abi Talib</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">caliph <a href="/years/656">656</a>–<a href="/years/661">661 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali ibn Abi Talib was the cousin and son-in-law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, ruling from 656 to 661 CE. His caliphate was marked by civil war, including conflict with Mu'awiya, who went on to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> after Ali's assassination. Shia Muslims regard him as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, and loyalty to his family later helped mobilise support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib.jpg" alt="Ali ibn Abi Talib" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: عبدالعزيز علي · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_ten_to_whom_Paradise_was_promised_(Ali).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to tradition Ali was born around 600 CE to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-talib-ibn-abd-al-muttalib">Abu Talib ibn Abd al-Muttalib</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> bint Asad of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a>, a clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and he may have been born inside the Ka'ba. When he was about five he came under the guardianship of Muhammad and Khadija. In 622, when Muhammad escaped a plot to kill him and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, Ali stayed behind as his decoy. Muhammad gave him his daughter Fatima in marriage, and Ali served as his secretary and deputy. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghadir-khumm">Ghadir Khumm</a> in 632 Muhammad declared, &quot;He whose mawla I am, Ali is his mawla&quot;, a saying whose meaning Shia and Sunni Muslims interpret differently.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Muhammad died in 632, a group of Muslims met at the Saqifa in Ali's absence and appointed Abu Bakr. Ali accepted this, probably for the sake of Muslim unity, though he considered himself the best qualified. He later withdrew from public life under the first three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. After Egyptian rebels killed Uthman in 656, Ali was offered the caliphate by the Iraqis, most of the Ansar and most of the Muhajirun, and he accepted after some hesitation.</p><p>He dismissed nearly all of Uthman's governors as corrupt and distributed the treasury equally, policies praised as righteous and criticised as naive. Talha, Zubayr and Muhammad's widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a> rebelled, and Ali defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel">Battle of the Camel</a> in 656. Mu'awiya, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, refused to step down, and the two fought the inconclusive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-siffin">Battle of Siffin</a> in 657. The arbitration that followed alienated a group of Ali's army known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>, whom he crushed at the Battle of Nahrawan in 658.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ali was struck with a poisoned sword during the morning prayer at the Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> on 28 January 661, by the Kharijite Abd al-Rahman ibn Muljam, in revenge for Nahrawan, and died about two days later. His son Hasan was acknowledged as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in Kufa but abdicated in August 661 to Mu'awiya, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. Alids were persecuted under the Umayyads and later under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. Ali's other son Husayn was killed by Umayyad forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> in 680.</p><p>Sunni Muslims regard Ali as the last of the rightly guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, while Shia Muslims venerate him as their first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> and Muhammad's rightful successor. Nahj al-balagha, a collection of sermons, letters and sayings attributed to him, was compiled in the eleventh century by Sharif al-Radi, and recent research attributes most of it to Ali.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Into this country came Idris ibn Abdallah, a great-grandson of Hasan ibn Ali and so a descendant of <b>Ali ibn Abi Talib</b> and Fatima, the daughter of the prophet Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ali ibn Abi Talib</b>’s troubled caliphate from 656 to 661 CE was dominated by the First Fitna, the civil war between Ali and the governor of Syria, Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, over responsibility for Uthman’s murder.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Umayyad Caliphate was established in 661 CE after the assassination of <b>Ali ibn Abi Talib</b>, the fourth of the Rashidun caliphs and the cousin and son-in-law of the Prophet Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kharijite Ibn Muljam struck the fourth caliph, <b>Ali ibn Abi Talib</b>, with a poisoned sword as he prayed in the Great Mosque of Kufa.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Siffin on the Euphrates, Caliph <b>Ali ibn Abi Talib</b> fought Muawiya, governor of Syria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Musha'sha held extreme (ghulat) Shia beliefs: Muhammad's son Mawla Ali claimed that <b>Ali ibn Abi Talib</b> was divine and sacked Najaf in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/state/mushasha">Musha'sha</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=659" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ali ibn Abi Talib"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=659">What was here in 659 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
